-- title:
--   Hecke compatibility of the supersingular residue pairing
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime with $p\ge 5$, let $K$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, and let $N,\ell$ be nonzero naturals with $\ell$ prime. Write $F=$ `modularFunctionFieldC K N`, the subfield of $K((q))$ generated by the $q$-expansions $j(q)$ and $j(q^N)$, and $R=$ `charLDegeneracyRoof K N ℓ`, generated by $j(q)$, $j(q^N)$, $j(q^{\ell})$ and $j(q^{N\ell})$; both are assumed to be curves over $K$ in the sense of `IsCurveOver` (principal divisors of degree zero, finite residue extensions, free rank-one Kähler module), to carry canonical local residues at every place, to have every place's uniformizer differential generating, and to have nontrivial Kähler modules, and $R$ is assumed to have principal divisors. Assume $(N:K)\neq 0$, $\ell\nmid N$, $\ell\neq p$, that $K^{\times}$ contains a primitive $\ell$-th root of unity $\zeta$, and let $m\ge 1$. Assume the two degeneracy maps $\alpha=$ `heckeAlphaC K N ℓ` (the inclusion $F\subseteq R$) and $\beta=$ `heckeBetaC K N ℓ` (induced by $q\mapsto q^{\ell}$) are integral. Let $SS$ be a finite set of places of $F$ whose members are exactly the supersingular places `ssPlaces p N K` (rational, affine geometric, with $j$-value in the supersingular set), and assume leg-stability: for every place $w$ of $R$, the restriction of $w$ along $\alpha$ lies in $SS$ if and only if its restriction along $\beta$ does. Let $v$ assign a scalar in $K$ to each element of `SSIndex p N K hp5 (2m)`, i.e. to each supersingular place $x$ with `placeWidth N x` dividing $m$, and let $\omega\in\Omega_{F/K}$ satisfy: at every such index place $x$, $\mathrm{ord}_x$ of the coefficient of $\omega$ with respect to $d\pi_x$ is at least $D_m(x)-1$, where $D_m=$ `weightDivisor K N m`; and at every $x\in SS$ with `placeWidth N x` not dividing $m$, that order is at least $D_m(x)$. Then, with $\tilde{\,\cdot\,}=$ `liftFun p N K hp5 (2m)` the chosen function with prescribed poles along $-D_m$ and prescribed leading coefficients, $T=$ `ssHeckeFun p N K hp5 (2m) ℓ` the supersingular Hecke operator on value vectors, and $h=$ `heckeMultiplier N K ℓ` the element of $R$ with $d(\beta j)=h\cdot \alpha^{*}dj$, one has
--   $$\sum_{x\in SS}\mathrm{Tr}_{\kappa(x)/K}\,\mathrm{res}_x\bigl(\widetilde{Tv}\cdot \partial_x\omega\bigr)=\sum_{z\in SS}\mathrm{Tr}_{\kappa(z)/K}\,\mathrm{res}_z\bigl(\tilde v\cdot\partial_z\,\mathrm{Tr}_{\beta}\bigl((\ell^{m-1}h^{m})\cdot\alpha^{*}\omega\bigr)\bigr),$$
--   where $\alpha^{*}$ and $\mathrm{Tr}_{\beta}$ denote `Differential.pullbackAlong` along $\alpha$ and `Differential.traceAlong` along $\beta$, each function being inserted through `diagonalHom` as the constant family of its value, and $\partial$ the coefficient with respect to the local uniformizer differential.
--
--   This is the adjunction (Hecke-equivariance) property of the supersingular residue pairing: summed over all supersingular places, pairing a differential against the Hecke transform of a value vector equals pairing the transposed correspondence $\mathrm{Tr}_{\beta}\circ(\ell^{m-1}h^{m}\cdot)\circ\alpha^{*}$ applied to the differential against the original vector. It is used by [`ModularCurve.ssResiduePairing_ssHeckeFun_eq_comp_traceAlong`](thm.html#ModularCurve.ssResiduePairing_ssHeckeFun_eq_comp_traceAlong) in the analysis of weights and mod $p$ Hecke actions on supersingular points.
